We have 8 drones in total. Since Field_4 has the highest threat level (0.24) and requires 3 drones for full protection, we keep the drones already closest to Field_4—Drone_3, Drone_4, and Drone_7—in that group. Next, Field_3 (threat 0.11) becomes the second priority and needs 2 drones. Of the remaining drones, the two closest (by approximating distances from their positions to Field_3’s area) are Drone_1 (originally protecting Field_1) and the idle Drone_6; thus we assign them to protect Field_3. That leaves Field_1 (threat 0.07) with the remaining three drones (Drone_2, Drone_5, and Drone_8). Although Field_1 ideally would have 4 drones for full protection, with only 8 drones available overall we choose to fully cover the highest‐threat fields and accept that Field_1 is underprotected. This distribution minimizes the overall expected damage by ensuring that the fields with higher threat levels (Field_4 and Field_3) receive complete protection with the closest drones.
